        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Good timing @justinmk, I was just starting to get back into some neovim backlog items I've had on my plate earlier this morning.\n\nAs for this one--the point here is that these change notifications now send potentially many heavy requests to LSP servers since they are all tied to the LspNotify didChange notification. Making this a rate limiter and sending one immediately after inactivity causes lots of requests whose responses we'll just discard anyway during active document churn.\n\nTwo things:\n1. Sending immediately even as a rate limiter doesn't make much sense to me. If I open a doc and start typing, the first new character will cause the didChange even though I just started typing. The initial debounce interval should probably elapse just to gather as many of the changes up that might happen. If I edit a doc and type all my changes inside the debounce interval, I only have to send one change notification, not two.\n\n2. didChange notifications every N milliseconds even during document churn could be useful for servers to stay in sync and reduce large changesets from accumulating--but we need to be smarter about what downstream effects this has. If we want to keep this as a rate limiter, I think we need a _separate, true_ debounce timer for all the LSP feature/capability requests.\n\nWhat we can consider instead is:\n\n- don't send a change notification immediately on the first edit after a long quiet period, continue to wait the interval\n- keep it as a rate limiter after that and flush changes every N milliseconds like it does now\n- change LSP capability framework code to use a separate debounce timer of its own that is triggered by the upstream change notification\n\nOn the last bullet--this provides the closest behavior neovim used to have before I refactored semantic tokens (and everything else) from using its own debounce based on buffer events. The new method still allows the module interface to provide the timer abstraction so individual features don't have to care, but we can be guaranteed that we won't send requests for document versions the server hasn't seen yet if the two get out of sync.\n\nThe hardest question is honestly how to configure (if we make it configurable) this \"feature debounce.\" I think helix actually uses separate times for each feature based on how heavy/prioritized the results are.\n\nThoughts?\n\nAlso, I've got a few more fixes I'm working on to harden some race conditions that currently exist in semantic tokens and buffer reloading due to filesystem events, which may touch some of this. ",
